<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="street-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="street-title">Find your street</h2>
      <p>Descriptions come from Wikipedia and a city guide, and each street holds other businesses too. Only the trades are from those sources. The accounting points are our general analysis.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="market-list">
      <article>
        <h3>Jewellery workshops and showrooms</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Wikipedia mentions a Bengali community mostly employed in jewellery manufacturing, and shopping streets such as Ajmal Khan Road.</p>
        <p><strong>What matters:</strong> gold given to a karigar is still yours until it comes back, so the register must be in weight and purity, not rupees alone. A showroom that also runs a workshop should keep the two records apart, or it cannot tell shop margin from making margin.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Tank Road: wholesale garments</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Wikipedia describes a wholesale garment market that began with a few shopkeepers in the late 1980s.</p>
        <p><strong>What matters:</strong> returns from retailers, season-end stock and any stitching done outside. {% routeLink "/locations/accounting-services-in-gandhi-nagar/", "The Gandhi Nagar page" %} follows a lot of cloth through the books, and most of it applies here.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Hardhyan Singh Road: leather</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">Wikipedia names a leather market here among the area's wholesale trades.</p>
        <p><strong>What matters:</strong> hides and finished goods sell by different units, so stock needs both. Ask how offcuts and rejects are recorded, and check the GST rate by product, not by category name.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Ghaffar Market: phones and repairs</h3>
        <p class="market-list__fact">A city guide describes electronics, second-hand phones and laptops, and many repair shops.</p>
        <p><strong>What matters:</strong> job cards that split labour from parts, and purchase records for used handsets. The next section covers these in detail.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="repair-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="repair-title">Running a phone repair and resale counter</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Issue a job card for each device, with labour and parts apart</li>
        <li>Note who you bought a used device from, what you paid, and its IMEI</li>
        <li>Keep small parts in simple groups, and count them regularly</li>
        <li>Match the day's cash and UPI to the jobs and sales recorded</li>
        <li>Note parts given free under your own repair warranty</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Margin-based GST on used goods</h2>
      <p>A used handset bought from a customer usually comes without any GST to claim. Rule 32(5) of the CGST Rules covers a person dealing in second-hand goods, used as such or after minor processing that does not change their nature. Where no input tax credit was taken on the purchase, the value of supply is the difference between selling and purchase prices, and a negative difference is ignored.</p>
      <p class="para">That is why the purchase record with the IMEI matters: without it, you cannot show the margin. Ask whether replacing a screen or battery still counts as minor processing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="karigar-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="karigar-title">Gold out with a karigar</h2>
      <p>Handing gold to a karigar to make jewellery can be job work. If you are registered and send inputs to a job worker without paying tax, section 143 of the CGST Act keeps responsibility for proper accounts with you. Inputs must come back, or be supplied from there, within one year. If not, they are treated as supplied by you on the day they were sent.</p>
      <p class="para">Ask how this applies to your arrangement, and agree an allowed loss with each karigar in writing, so a shortfall can be explained later.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>About the area, and about us</h2>
      <p>Workshops and repair counters here often keep karigar registers and job cards on paper. Agree at the start how copies will be made and sent each month, so the books do not wait for someone to collect them.</p>
      <p class="para">AccountOrbit has no office in Karol Bagh, and takes enquiries by phone from anywhere in Delhi. See {% routeLink "/guides/how-to-hire-an-accountant-in-delhi/", "how to hire an accountant in Delhi" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

